 ## Overview
-Hard and Soft Depth Regularization is a geometric constraint strategy for 3D Gaussian Splatting that prevents detail loss and blurred appearance by decoupling the optimization of Gaussian centers from their opacities [S1]. It sets itself apart from standard depth regularization methods (which often deform Gaussian shapes to fit smooth monocular depth) by freezing scale and rotation parameters, thereby encouraging spatial reshaping of primitives rather than shape deformation [S1]. This approach balances coarse depth fitting with fine color appearance preservation [S1].
+Hard and Soft Depth Regularization is a geometric constraint strategy for 3D Gaussian Splatting that prevents detail loss and blurred appearance by decoupling the optimization of Gaussian centers and opacities from their shape parameters. It solves the problem where standard depth regularization forces anisotropic Gaussian shapes to conform to smooth monocular depth estimates, which conflicts with complex color appearance [S1]. It sets itself apart from sibling methods like [Frequency Regularization](6130-frequency-regularization.md) and [3D Half-Gaussian Splatting Covariance Transformation and Regularization](9150-3d-half-gaussian-splatting-covariance-transformation-and-reg.md) by explicitly freezing scale and rotation to enforce spatial reshaping (movement of centers) rather than shape deformation [S1].
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs:**
-- Current set of 3D Gaussians, each defined by center $\mu$, scale $s$, rotation quaternion $q$, opacity $\alpha$, and spherical harmonics coefficients for color.
-- Ground-truth depth maps $D_{gt}$ corresponding to training views.
-- Ground-truth RGB images $I_{gt}$.
